course: dictionary
lesson: 153
title: Afloat: on the water or still going
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Afloat means floating on water, not sinking. «In a boat or canoe always wear a life jacket that fits and stay seated, because boats can tip over and a life jacket keeps you afloat even when you are tired or hurt.» The clue is the life jacket: it keeps you on top of the water, so afloat means floating.
explain: Afloat can also mean on a boat or ship at sea. A sailor might say the ship stayed afloat through the storm, meaning it did not sink.
explain: Afloat has a second, figurative meaning. If a person or business stays afloat, they have just enough money to keep going and not fail. Ask yourself: is the sentence about water or about money? The words around it tell you which.
fact: Afloat means floating on water, not sinking.
fact: Afloat can also mean on a boat or ship at sea.
fact: Afloat usually comes after a verb, as in «stay afloat» or «keep afloat».
fact: A life jacket keeps you afloat in water.
fact: Figurative means not literal: «keep the business afloat» means keep it from failing.
exercise: «In a boat or canoe always wear a life jacket that fits, because a life jacket keeps you afloat.» What does afloat mean here? | Very tired and sleepy | Dry and warm in the sun | Sinking to the bottom of the lake | *Floating on water, not sinking
exercise: The family shop was in trouble, but its new customers helped it stay afloat. What does afloat mean? | Floating on a lake in summer | *Still going, and not failing | Painted bright blue | Sunk to the bottom of the sea
exercise: Which word means floating on water, not sinking? | sunk | heavy | drowned | *afloat
test: «The sailors worked all night to keep the leaking ship afloat.» Why did they work all night? | To paint the ship a new colour | To make the ship go faster | *To stop the ship sinking | To go to sleep on the deck
test: A sailor says, «We have been afloat for three weeks.» What does afloat mean? | Lost in a forest | *At sea on a ship | Stuck in a cupboard | Very late for school
exam: Which sentence uses afloat to mean not failing? | *The café stayed afloat through the hard winter | The lid was afloat in the bath | The leaves were afloat in the wind | The duck was afloat on the pond
